Package: cleanTMLE
Title: Software Layer for an Outcome-Blind Staged Targeted-Learning Workflow
Version: 0.3.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Andrew", "Mertens", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           email = "amertens@berkeley.edu"),
    person("cleanTMLE Contributors", role = "ctb"))
Description: Software infrastructure for an outcome-blind staged workflow
    for targeted-learning observational causal analyses on binary
    point-treatment studies. The package implements the staged-analysis
    framework of Muntner et al. (2024) by recording prespecification,
    design diagnostics, and a structured pre-outcome decision before the
    treatment-outcome association is examined. The tested scope covers
    binary point exposure, binary outcome, and the marginal
    risk-difference estimand, with three estimator families: propensity-
    score matching, stabilised inverse-probability-of-treatment weighting,
    and targeted minimum loss-based estimation (TMLE), including
    simulation-selected TMLE via SuperLearner ensemble learning.
    The first methodological contribution is an outcome-blind plasmode
    candidate-selection procedure in which nuisance-parameter strategies
    are evaluated by oracle coverage on simulated outcomes before real
    outcome access. The second is a prespecified data-quality stress test
    ('stress_test') that evaluates each candidate under covariate-
    missingness, treatment-misclassification, outcome-misclassification,
    unmeasured-confounding, and near-positivity scenarios, producing a
    degradation gradient on the same performance scale as the baseline
    plasmode metrics and, when decision thresholds were declared at lock
    creation, a locked verdict with tipping points. Together, the two
    screens produce a pre-outcome study dossier that a review team can
    examine before authorising the primary analysis. Time-to-event
    helpers (IPW risk curves, g-computation, augmented IPW, IPW hazard
    ratios, survival TMLE via 'survtmle', and longitudinal TMLE via
    'lmtp') are experimental extensions outside the tested scope.
